DESCRIPTION:Location Link: Central Library<br>Room Location: Level 8 - G
 allery<br>Registration Required: Registration is not required.<br>Detail
 ed Description: <img src="https://www.trumba.com/i/DgBohZRAtMKeE3LJerr2C
 -0L.png" alt=" Image of a label with the words: About this event" width=
 "147" height="77">\nBattle for Paradise is a photo essay by <a href="htt
 ps://www.spl.org/programs-and-services/arts-and-culture/artist-in-reside
 nce" dir="ltr">Jo Cosme</a> that examines the tension between Puerto Ric
 o as a marketed paradise and Puerto Rico as a lived reality. Part of the
  ongoing project Welcome to Paradise: ¡Viva Puerto Rico Libre!\, the wor
 k focuses on how tourism\, settler colonialism\, and disaster-driven pol
 icies reshape who can remain on the archipelago and under what condition
 s.\n\nThrough photography\, Cosme highlights the contrast between images
  designed to attract and the realities often kept out of frame—restricte
 d access to land and beaches to the native population\, rising costs of 
 living\, and the displacement of local communities. The phrase “restrict
 ions apply” points to the fine print behind the promise of paradise\, wh
 ere access is uneven and shaped by power.\n\nRather than presenting Puer
 to Rico as a distant place\, the work asks viewers to consider their pro
 ximity to these systems and what it means to move through them\, whether
  as tourists\, settlers\, investors\, or witnesses.\n\nThanks to our spo
 nsor&nbsp\;<a href="https://supportspl.org/" target="_blank" dir="ltr">T
 he Seattle Public Library Foundation</a>.\n\nFree and reduced access sup
 ported by&nbsp\;<a href="https://www.4culture.org/" target="_blank" rel=
 "noopener" dir="ltr">4Culture</a>.\n<img src="https://www.trumba.com/i/D
 gCMPU7fjf4bRpZl1rfOD36M.png" alt="Information" width="147" height="77">
 \nThis exhibit will run from Monday\, October 5 to Sunday\, December 6\, 
 2026\, at our Level 8 Gallery.\n\nLearn more about <a href="https://www.
 spl.org/programs-and-services/arts-and-culture/exhibits" dir="ltr">Exhib
 its</a> at the Library.\n\nLearn about our<a href="https://www.spl.org/p
 rograms-and-services/arts-and-culture/artist-in-residence" dir="ltr"> Ar
 tist in Residence program</a>.<br>Contact Us: <img alt=" Image of a labe
 l with the words: Contact us" height="77" src="https://www.trumba.com/i/
 DgB32CYHnGABZQ0H91yPaGlC.png" width="147" />\nIf you have questions abou
 t this event\, <a href="https://www.spl.org/ask">Ask Us</a> or call 206-
 386-4636.<br>ADA Accommodations: <img alt=" Image of a label with the wo
 rd: Accessibility" height="77" src="https://www.trumba.com/i/DgDwSTCO4qE
 McIjTU7r7EVN4.png" width="147" />\nWe can provide accommodations for peo
 ple with disabilities at Library events. Please <a href="https://www.spl
 .org/using-the-library/accessibility/americans-with-disabilities-act/ada
 -accommodation-request" target="_blank">contact&nbsp\;LEAP</a> at least 
 seven days before the event to request accommodations.\n\nAll Library pr
 ograms are free and open to the public.&nbsp\;&nbsp\;\n&nbsp\;<br><br>Vi
 ew a photo essay by Artist in Residence\, Jo Cosme\, that examines the t
 ension between Puerto Rico as a marketed paradise and Puerto Rico as a l
 ived reality.
